BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Poetic Medicine in Community: Healing Through Creativity and Conne
 ction: 11/10/26 — Sebastopol Center For the Arts\, California
DTSTART:20261110T000000
DTEND:20261111T000000
UID:f733a46a55634224dc8006205f8a480f@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Poetic Medicine in Community: Healing Through Creativity and C
 onnection: November 10th\, 2026\n\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          $
 15.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nPoetic Medicine in Community: Healing
  Through Creativity and ConnectionTuesday\, November 10th\, 4:00-5:30pm Cl
 ass Description: Poetry can be a salve for the heart and soul. It is a way
  of speaking what is true in our hearts and is often difficult to access w
 ithout a creative spark. You do not have to be a “poet” or even a “w
 riter” to allow words and feeling to your through your pen. COME JOIN US
 !Writing poetry in community is a bit like attending a meditation retreat.
  The instruction for most beginners when learning to meditate is to simply
  bring oneself back to the breath\, each time the mind wanders. In poem-ma
 king\, the instruction is to bring oneself back to the heart and along wit
 h a deep breath\, see if one can open a little bit wider. When we witness 
 each other in this process\, in a circle of open-heartedness\, something m
 agical happens!This is an IN-PERSON gathering in an open and safe creative
  community. We are all poets! Please bring your own writing materials. 22 
 students maximum. About the Instructor: Redwing Keyssar has been leading 
 “Poetic Medicine in Community” sessions through her work in Palliative
  Care at UCSF since 2020. Her work has created an international community 
 of people who\, by listening to and writing poetry together\, have healed 
 wounds of grief and loss\, helped ”wounded healers\,” and provided res
 iliency to move forward on our life paths.Redwing is a Palliative Care nur
 se\, educator and consultant\, an end-of-life doula\, author\, poet\, song
 -writer\, ceremonialist\, national presenter and frequent contributor to p
 ublic issues about Palliative and End of Life Care. She is a Board member 
 of the Sebastopol Senior Center and offers presentations about end-of-life
  issues throughout the Bay Area. Before working at the UCSF MERI CENTER\, 
 her professional career included being the Clinical Director at Zen Hospic
 e Project and the Director of Palliative Care and Nursing at Jewish Family
  and Children’s Services from 2004-2018. She is a founder and adjunct fa
 culty member of the California State University Shiley/Haynes Institute of
  Palliative Care and has taught courses at OLLI at Sonoma State. Redwing i
 s the author of an award-winning book\, “Last Acts of Kindness\; Lessons
  for the Living from the Bedsides of the Dying” and is the recipient of 
 numerous awards in the field of Palliative Care.She is passionate about ut
 ilizing poetry as a healing modality!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\
 n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n              \n   
              Add to cart
LOCATION:Sebastopol Center for the Arts\, 282 S High St\, Sebastopol\, CA 
 95472
URL:https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/mswjqmpbfvv5tzy2ls34e2u8tcadn
 r
X-SOURCE:Sebastopol Center for the Arts
END:VEVENT
