BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Poetic Medicine in Community: Healing Through Creativity and Conne
 ction: 12/8/2026 — Sebastopol Center For the Arts\, California
DTSTART:20261208T000000
DTEND:20261209T000000
UID:3d3af6eaa1215cd7926ced4a23b3e5d6@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Poetic Medicine in Community: Healing Through Creativity and C
 onnection: December 8th\, 2026\n\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          $1
 5.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nPoetic Medicine in Community: Healing 
 Through Creativity and ConnectionTuesday\, December 8th\, 4:00-5:30pm Clas
 s Description: Poetry can be a salve for the heart and soul. It is a way o
 f speaking what is true in our hearts and is often difficult to access wit
 hout a creative spark. You do not have to be a “poet” or even a “wri
 ter” to allow words and feeling to your through your pen. COME JOIN US!W
 riting poetry in community is a bit like attending a meditation retreat. T
 he instruction for most beginners when learning to meditate is to simply b
 ring oneself back to the breath\, each time the mind wanders. In poem-maki
 ng\, the instruction is to bring oneself back to the heart and along with 
 a deep breath\, see if one can open a little bit wider. When we witness ea
 ch other in this process\, in a circle of open-heartedness\, something mag
 ical happens!This is an IN-PERSON gathering in an open and safe creative c
 ommunity. We are all poets! Please bring your own writing materials. 22 st
 udents maximum. About the Instructor: Redwing Keyssar has been leading “
 Poetic Medicine in Community” sessions through her work in Palliative Ca
 re at UCSF since 2020. Her work has created an international community of 
 people who\, by listening to and writing poetry together\, have healed wou
 nds of grief and loss\, helped ”wounded healers\,” and provided resili
 ency to move forward on our life paths.Redwing is a Palliative Care nurse\
 , educator and consultant\, an end-of-life doula\, author\, poet\, song-wr
 iter\, ceremonialist\, national presenter and frequent contributor to publ
 ic issues about Palliative and End of Life Care. She is a Board member of 
 the Sebastopol Senior Center and offers presentations about end-of-life is
 sues throughout the Bay Area. Before working at the UCSF MERI CENTER\, her
  professional career included being the Clinical Director at Zen Hospice P
 roject and the Director of Palliative Care and Nursing at Jewish Family an
 d Children’s Services from 2004-2018. She is a founder and adjunct facul
 ty member of the California State University Shiley/Haynes Institute of Pa
 lliative Care and has taught courses at OLLI at Sonoma State. Redwing is t
 he author of an award-winning book\, “Last Acts of Kindness\; Lessons fo
 r the Living from the Bedsides of the Dying” and is the recipient of num
 erous awards in the field of Palliative Care.She is passionate about utili
 zing poetry as a healing modality!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n
 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n              \n      
           Add to cart
LOCATION:Sebastopol Center for the Arts\, 282 S High St\, Sebastopol\, CA 
 95472
URL:https://www.sebarts.org/classes-lectures/poetic-medicine-1282026
X-SOURCE:Sebastopol Center for the Arts
END:VEVENT
